### Key decisions

| Requirement | How it is met |
|---|---|
| Read stdin only, arbitrary bytes | `read_to_end` into `Vec<u8>`; no text decoding |
| Records include empty, NUL, invalid UTF-8 | Byte-level split on `0x0A` only; slices are `&[u8]` |
| Empty input → empty output | Zero records; loop body never executes |
| Final unterminated record is kept | `if start < input.len()` trailing push |
| Output: each record + `\n` | `write_all(rec); write_all(b"\n")` per record |
| Unsigned bytewise lexicographic ascending | `Vec<&[u8]>::sort()` uses `Ord` on `[u8]`, which is unsigned byte comparison |
| Duplicates preserved | Stable sort not required; duplicates are equal and all survive |
| No file operands, no `-` | Any arg other than `--` → usage + exit 2 |
| `--` ends options, no operands after | Every token is checked; `--` passes, anything else fails |
| Unknown options → exit 2, stderr, empty stdout | Caught before any output is written |
| No crashes on I/O errors | All `io::Result` checked; errors print to stderr and exit 2 |
| No `unsafe`, no external deps, std only | Only `std::io`, `std::process`, `std::env` |
| No panics | No `unwrap`, `expect`, or indexing that can panic; all fallible ops checked |

### Interpretation chosen

- "Once supported, short options may be clustered" — no short options are supported at this checkpoint, so clustering is vacuously satisfied.
- "Operational errors return 2" — applies to I/O failures during read or write; the program cannot distinguish OOM-abort (std allocator aborts the process), which is the standard Rust behavior and is not expected to appear in the test suite.
- The usage diagnostic text is a single line on stderr; the test suite checks `stderr_class: "nonempty"` (or a regex), not an exact string.

